RKLLM runtime for Debian / Ubuntu on Rockchip boards
===================================================

What is installed
-----------------
  @LIBDIR_ABS@/librkllmrt.so      runtime shared library (SONAME: librkllmrt.so)
  /usr/include/@PKGINCLUDE_SUBDIR@/rkllm.h    public C/C++ API header
  /usr/include/rkllm.h            compatibility symlink to the header above
  @LIBDIR_ABS@/pkgconfig/rkllm.pc pkg-config metadata
  @LIBDIR_ABS@/cmake/rkllm/*.cmake CMake package configuration
  /usr/bin/rkllm                  local chat, one-shot inference and HTTP API
  /usr/lib/@PACKAGE@/librknnrt.so private copy of the RKNN runtime needed by the
                                  multimodal example (kept out of the multiarch
                                  path so it can never clash with a distro or
                                  vendor rknn runtime package)
  /usr/include/rknn/*.h           RKNN API headers used by that example
  /etc/ld.so.conf.d/@PACKAGE@.conf declares /usr/lib/@PACKAGE@ to ldconfig
  /usr/bin/rkllm-fix-freq-rk*     board tuning helpers (CPU/NPU/DDR frequency
                                  lock and performance watch)
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/@SERVER_UNIT@
                                  optional API service unit (not enabled)
  @SERVER_DEFAULTS@               configuration for that unit (a dpkg conffile)
  /usr/share/@PACKAGE@/examples/  upstream example sources (read-only)
  /usr/share/man/man1/rkllm.1.gz  manual page (English)
  /usr/share/man/zh_CN/man1/rkllm.1.gz
                                  manual page (simplified Chinese)
  /usr/share/doc/@PACKAGE@/       SDK PDFs, upstream README/benchmark/changelog

Manual pages
------------
  `man rkllm` picks the language from your locale. To force one:

    man rkllm                 # English, or Chinese when LANG=zh_CN.UTF-8
    man -L zh_CN rkllm        # simplified Chinese
    man -L en rkllm           # English

Quick start: run a model and talk to it
---------------------------------------
  The package does NOT contain a model - see "Getting a model" below. Once a
  .rkllm file is on the board:

    rkllm list                              # where does it look for models?
    rkllm chat qwen2.5-1.5b                 # interactive chat (REPL)
    rkllm chat /data/models/qwen2.5-1.5b.rkllm
    rkllm run  qwen2.5-1.5b "hello"         # one-shot, script friendly
    rkllm info                              # driver / library / model report
    rkllm selftest                          # verify the installation

  Inside the REPL: /help, /clear (drop the KV cache), /set temperature 0.7,
  /exit. Ctrl-C interrupts generation.

  rkllm is a plain Python 3 script; it talks to librkllmrt.so through ctypes.
  There is nothing to compile on the board and nothing to install with pip.

Serving an OpenAI-compatible API
--------------------------------
  Ad hoc, in the foreground:

    rkllm serve qwen2.5-1.5b --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080
    curl http://localhost:8080/v1/chat/completions \
      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
      -d '{"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"hi"}],"stream":true}'

  As a service (start it only after choosing a model):

    sed -i 's/^RKLLM_MODEL=.*/RKLLM_MODEL=qwen2.5-1.5b/' @SERVER_DEFAULTS@
    systemctl enable --now @SERVER_UNIT@

  Endpoints: GET /health, GET /v1/models, POST /v1/chat/completions
  (streaming SSE, sampling parameters, tool calling), POST /v1/reset.

  The NPU can serve exactly one request at a time. Extra requests queue up to
  --max-queue (default 32) and are refused with HTTP 503 beyond that, with a
  Retry-After header. The unit deliberately carries no ConditionPathExists: no
  single path reliably means "the NPU works" (see Runtime requirements below),
  so whether the NPU is usable is decided by the runtime at startup, and
  StartLimitBurst stops a board without an NPU from restarting forever.

  Authentication: `rkllm serve --api-key <secret>` (or $RKLLM_API_KEY) makes every
  endpoint require "Authorization: Bearer <secret>"; requests without it get
  HTTP 401. Prefer the environment variable over the flag, because a value on
  the command line is visible in `ps` and `systemctl status`. For the service,
  put RKLLM_API_KEY in @SERVER_DEFAULTS@ and `chmod 600` that file. The API is
  plain HTTP, so the key only keeps stray clients out - if the board is reachable
  from an untrusted network, terminate TLS in front of it.

  Clients configure the base URL as http://<host>:<port>/v1. Endpoints are also
  accepted without the /v1 prefix. If this server has no key configured, the
  client's key field is ignored - fill it with any non-empty placeholder rather
  than leaving it blank, some clients refuse to send otherwise.

Service configuration
---------------------
  @SERVER_DEFAULTS@ configures the service, and it is a dpkg *conffile*:

    * Local edits survive an upgrade. The text of the file does not depend on
      the package version, so a newer package does not rewrite it, and the file
      mode is left alone too - a `chmod 600` applied because RKLLM_API_KEY lives
      in it stays in force.
    * It is deleted when the package is removed, a plain `apt remove` included,
      because this package promises to leave nothing behind. Copy it aside
      first if you want those settings back.
    * If dpkg asks whether to install the maintainer's version of this file,
      keep yours: the shipped one is the untouched template with RKLLM_MODEL
      empty, so accepting it loses your model and, if set, your API key.

  Every `rkllm serve` parameter has its own entry there, documented in English
  and in Chinese, each with an optional example that is commented out - from
  RKLLM_MODEL and RKLLM_HOST/RKLLM_PORT/RKLLM_API_KEY through the context
  window, sampling parameters and queue limits, down to RKLLM_EXTRA_ARGS as the
  escape hatch. `rkllm serve --help` lists the same options from the other side.

  Apply a change with `systemctl restart @SERVER_UNIT@`. The file is read by
  systemd (EnvironmentFile=), not by a shell: one KEY=value per line, `#` only
  at the beginning of a line, quote values containing spaces, no $VAR expansion,
  and an empty value means "use the built-in default".

  The full 82-key table (queue limits, prefix cache, MCP, logging, ...) is NOT
  in that file - it would be unreadable as KEY=value. Generate a documented TOML
  template instead and edit what you need:

    rkllm config init --board --path /etc/rkllm/rkllm.toml
    rkllm config docs                  # the same 83 keys as a Markdown table
    rkllm config show                  # effective values + where each came from

  @SERVER_DEFAULTS@ remains the only file the unit reads; /etc/rkllm/rkllm.toml
  is picked up automatically when it exists (or point RKLLM_TOML at another
  path). Priority: built-in default < TOML < RKLLM_* environment < command line.

Getting a model
---------------
  The runtime executes .rkllm files only. They are produced on an x86_64 host,
  not on the board:

    1. On the x86 host, install the toolkit wheel matching your Python version
       (from the rkllm-toolkit directory of the release archive, cp310/cp311/
       cp312 are provided).
    2. Convert the HuggingFace checkpoint with the upstream export script:
         /usr/share/@PACKAGE@/examples/rkllm_api_demo/export/export_rkllm.py
       (point your quantised/calibrated weights at it; see the upstream README
       in the same directory for the arguments).
    3. Copy the resulting .rkllm file to the board. `rkllm` resolves a bare
       model name against: $RKLLM_MODEL_DIR, /usr/share/@PACKAGE@/models,
       /var/lib/rkllm/models, ~/.local/share/rkllm/models and the current
       directory - and it also accepts an absolute path. None of those
       directories is created by this package (so that removal stays spotless),
       make the one you prefer first, for example:

         mkdir -p /var/lib/rkllm/models
         cp qwen2.5-1.5b.rkllm /var/lib/rkllm/models/
         rkllm chat qwen2.5-1.5b

  Note that .rknn (vision) and .rkllm (language) are different formats; the
  multimodal example is the only part of this package that consumes .rknn.

Runtime requirements
--------------------
  * The rknpu kernel driver must already be present on the board (this package
    does NOT ship or touch it, and does not build DKMS modules). Run
    `rkllm info` to see whether it is reachable.

    Important: drivers from 0.9.x onwards do NOT create /dev/rknpu. The NPU is
    registered as a DRM render node, so check with

      sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version
      ls -l /sys/class/drm/renderD*/device/driver     # should point at rknpu

    instead of looking for a device node. `rkllm info` performs these checks for
    you and also accepts the legacy /dev/rknpu node if an older driver provides it.
  * librkllmrt.so needs libgomp (OpenMP), libstdc++ and glibc >= 2.29, which are
    pulled in as package dependencies.
  * /usr/bin/rkllm needs python3, which is a declared dependency.
  * Raising the clocks needs root; rkllm calls the rkllm-fix-freq-* helper when
    it runs as root and skips it otherwise (use --no-freq-lock to opt out).

Building a C/C++ program against the runtime
--------------------------------------------
  # plain compiler flags
  gcc demo.c -o demo -I/usr/include/@PKGINCLUDE_SUBDIR@ -lrkllmrt

  # pkg-config
  gcc demo.c -o demo $(pkg-config --cflags --libs rkllm)

  # CMake
  find_package(rkllm REQUIRED)
  target_link_libraries(demo PRIVATE rkllm::rkllmrt)

Building the C++ examples
-------------------------
  The example sources live under a read-only system directory, so copy the tree
  you want into a writable location first:

    cp -r /usr/share/@PACKAGE@/examples/rkllm_api_demo ~/rkllm_api_demo
    cd ~/rkllm_api_demo/deploy
    ./build-linux.sh          # native gcc/g++ build, output in ./build/

  The multimodal example needs OpenCV: it uses the system OpenCV when available,
  otherwise falls back to the copy bundled under
  /usr/share/@PACKAGE@/examples/multimodal_model_demo/deploy/3rdparty/opencv/.
  Install it with `apt install libopencv-dev` if you prefer the system one.

  The upstream Flask/Gradio server demos need python3-flask / gradio and are
  unrelated to /usr/bin/rkllm; see their own README in the examples directory.

Removing the package
--------------------
  apt remove @PACKAGE@   -- or --   dpkg -r @PACKAGE@

  Every installed path is listed in /var/lib/dpkg/info/@PACKAGE@.list. Nothing is
  written to /usr/local, no models are copied anywhere, no users are created and
  no DKMS modules are built. postinst/postrm only refresh the ld.so cache and
  tell systemd to re-read unit files; prerm stops the unit and postrm drops any
  enable symlink an operator created, so no dangling links survive.
  The service configuration @SERVER_DEFAULTS@ is deleted as well - not just by
  `apt purge` but by a plain `apt remove`, which dpkg would otherwise not do for
  a conffile. Back it up before removing the package if you want to keep the
  settings.
  The ld.so cache is refreshed on removal as well, so `ldconfig -p | grep rkllm`
  reports nothing afterwards.
